1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,500
Archaeological sites get looted once
their exact location is known.

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:11,500
Today one administrator can read it:
one leak exposes every site.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:19,200
The dig lead seals the true coordinates:
only ciphertext is kept, plus a 50 km blur.

4
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:25,600
Custodian Vault: no single administrator
can open a site alone.

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,800
A researcher sees only the blur.
Revealing the real position is denied.

6
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:37,800
So the researcher files a request
with a reason.

7
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:46,500
The community custodian approves:
1 of 2 approvals. One steward is not enough.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,500
The researcher is still locked out.

9
00:00:54,300 --> 00:01:05,500
The heritage authority, a second
independent steward, approves.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,800
2 of 3 agree: the role is granted
and the exact coordinates appear.

11
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:33,500
Revoking access also takes 2 of 3
approvals, never one person alone.

12
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:41,500
Once revoked, the researcher is
locked out again.

13
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,500
Every step is in the audit trail.

14
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:50,400
As Plan C allows, this demo is simulated.

15
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:55,200
Where TideCloak fits: sign-in, roles,
2-of-3 approvals, browser encryption.

16
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:58,000
With TideCloak live, nobody holds a key.
